\viewkind4\pard\cf1\fs32\b Calculate MTU probe sizes and interpret silence\b0\fs22\par\par
\pard\sa120 MTU measures an IP packet, including its IP header. Ping's data size is only part of that packet. Account for the headers before comparing a probe with an MTU.\par
\pard\li240\ri240\sa120\f1 Plain IPv4: 1,472 data + 8 ICMP + 20 IPv4 = 1,500 bytes.\par
Plain IPv6: 1,452 data + 8 ICMPv6 + 40 IPv6 = 1,500 bytes.\par
IPv4 options or IPv6 extension headers add more bytes.\f0\par
\pard\sa120 An IPv4 path-size test must set Don't Fragment and verify the actual behaviour. IPv6 routers do not fragment packets, but the source can: a large Echo Reply alone does not prove an unfragmented packet of that size crossed the path.\par
\pard\sa120 If an unfragmented 1,500-byte test succeeds and a 1,501-byte test times out, the result is only a tested lower bound of 1,500. Silence is inconclusive; it is not an exact MTU measurement. A matched packet-too-big response provides different evidence.\par
\pard\sa120 The planned tool distinguishes interface MTU, Windows-reported path MTU, tested successful sizes and an observed path estimate. If the backend cannot establish IPv6 source-fragmentation behaviour, it reports that limitation instead of a false exact number.\par
\pard\sa120 \cf2\ul RFC 8201 \u8212? IPv6 Path MTU Discovery\ul0\cf1  (online: \cf2\ul https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8201.html\ul0\cf1 )\par
